<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01379#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme is designed for viewers who are likely to interpret the image through the lens of Western media representations of Africa, particularly those who are familiar with or have been exposed to charity tourism and the 'white savior' narrative. The target audience includes individuals who may be emotionally responsive to appeals for global aid or who are accustomed to seeing images of 'helpless African children' as a justification for charitable giving or moral obligation. The meme leverages cultural markers such as the contrast in skin tone, the framing of the American teenage girl as an outsider, and the text overlay 'visiting the children of Africa' to activate stereotypes and emotional responses that are common in Western contexts. This audience is likely to be drawn to the image because it aligns with pre-existing narratives about global inequality and the perceived need for Western intervention, even if the meme's intent is to evoke pity, guilt, or inspiration without critically examining the power dynamics involved."@en .
