<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#assertion_about_01295> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#hasToxicityExplanation> "The meme uses false and dangerous accusations to threaten and abuse specific target communities (clergy and transgender individuals) by implying they are pedophiles and child molesters, which is a form of abuse targeting their identities."@en .
_:Nbdf5eccc18c74ee985f4d717f24a8c93 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:Nf694d17d621c4cfcbd4acf925a9bc4c0 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#playfulness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The squirrel's alert, curious expression with a slightly tilted head and direct gaze at the camera conveys a sense of playful engagement with its environment or the viewer. This is amplified by the humorous text overlay, which frames the squirrel's natural behavior as 'nuts' in a lighthearted, non-threatening way."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01436#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the visual material of the frog, which is used as a metaphorical stand-in for the autistic kid described in the text. The frog's physical resilience and unharmed state are visually aligned with the fictional 'tard strength' concept from the text, creating a semiotic projection where the viewer imagines themselves or identifies with the frog's metaphorical role in the absurd scenario. This projection is implicit, not explicit (e.g., no 'you' or 'me' deictic), but is activated by the meme's structure which maps the frog onto the autistic kid's fictional invulnerability."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#pun_on_nuts> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relatedTo> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#text_chunk_1> .
