<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a man dressed in clerical attire (red robe and white hat) sitting alone in a row of empty chairs, with overlaid text that reads: 'men wearing dresses and molesting our children?' and 'the word you're looking for is clergy, not transgender.' The overall intent is to use the visual of a clergyman juxtaposed with the text to imply that all clergy are pedophiles or molesters, and to mock or misdirect the term 'transgender' by suggesting it is a misnomer for 'clergy.' This meme is harmful and hateful as it targets religious figures (specifically clergy) by associating them with child molestation, which is a serious and false accusation. It also uses the term 'transgender' in a derogatory and misleading way, potentially contributing to transphobic sentiment by conflating gender identity with pedophilia. The meme is propagandistic in nature, aiming to influence public opinion against clergy and transgender individuals by creating a false equivalence and using fear-based rhetoric. The visual of the solitary clergyman in an empty hall reinforces a sense of isolation and guilt, amplifying the toxic message. The meme is also cyberbullying, as it attacks a specific group (clergy) based on their identity and role, and it is abusive in its implication of widespread criminal behavior. The meme's intent is to provoke outrage and reinforce negative stereotypes about religious institutions and transgender people, potentially causing harm to both groups by stigmatizing them and encouraging prejudice."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01527#assertion_about_01527> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01527#overallintent_instance> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#metaphoricallyMapsTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
_:N3c2f56d3328c4f41a1fffde9ff58a51c <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01327#Cynicism> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractionTimestamp> "2025-12-12T02:02:05.270868" .
_:Nab1362b9a783473fa229c356dac41fc9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#motionBlurIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:N305ff94baf144304884d212be4dd48e6 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01395#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_file_name; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a woman in labor in a hospital bed, visibly in pain, while a man (likely her partner) holds her hand. The overlaid text reads: 'i just found out mexican babies come out just like the food.' This meme uses the visual of childbirth — a universally recognized and often intense experience — to make a crude, stereotypical joke about Mexican people, implying that their babies are 'spicy' or 'hot' like Mexican food. This is a form of racial stereotyping and cultural appropriation, reducing a complex cultural identity to a joke about food. The intent is to provoke laughter through offensive and reductive humor, which can be considered harmful and mysogynous (as it targets a specific ethnic group in a demeaning way) and potentially abusive or hateful depending on context. The meme relies on the juxtaposition of the image of childbirth (a vulnerable, intense moment) with the absurd and offensive text to create a punchline rooted in prejudice. The visual elements (the woman's pain, the hospital setting) are used to amplify the shock value of the stereotype, making the joke more offensive. This type of content can perpetuate harmful stereotypes and contribute to a hostile environment for Mexican individuals and communities."@en .
_:Nd636fdb0911743ecaeae97c04ec8bfb7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:Nad45e2d8070546f4b97ba54fc48ca121 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:Na7752e266b9e4052a219dbd6fac88521 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#opaqueOverlaidOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N9e8c4f2e9e07456e9ad5522d8e0094e3 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#toxic_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme relies on toxic stereotypes that are harmful and dangerous. The stereotype that all clergy are pedophiles or molesters is a false and dangerous stereotype that has been used historically to discredit religious institutions and individuals. The stereotype that transgender people are pedophiles or molesters is a false and dangerous stereotype that has been used historically to discredit transgender people. This is a form of hate speech and cyberbullying."@en .
