_:N56064ef12baf4af3a25654df17c9b5a3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:N97fd7aa1eed04538b0a911737bf4fd88 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme is designed to target individuals who hold negative views or prejudices against religious clergy and transgender people, particularly those who may be influenced by or propagate harmful stereotypes. The intended audience likely includes people who are familiar with or sensitive to religious institutions (specifically Catholic clergy, given the attire) and/or those who are opposed to or fearful of transgender identities. The meme's use of false equivalence and hate speech aims to provoke outrage and reinforce existing biases, making it particularly effective in communities that already harbor animosity toward these groups. The visual of the isolated clergyman and the text's accusatory tone are designed to resonate with audiences who are predisposed to fear or distrust religious figures and/or transgender individuals."@en .
_:N4c36608e72d748fdabc3353105a14cd7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
_:N5088156262824bba9bd93ac47d98ea04 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:Nb80551d126d24670b7e0574df08d6403 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01327#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The target audience consists of fans of the Batman franchise, particularly those familiar with 'The Dark Knight' and Heath Ledger's portrayal of the Joker. The meme relies on cultural recognition of this specific cinematic character and his iconic, chaotic persona to deliver its darkly humorous message. The audience is expected to understand the Joker as a symbol of moral ambiguity and psychological intensity, which is central to the meme's intent. The humor style (dark humor, self-deprecating humor, irony) is also a key community marker, indicating the target audience likely appreciates or engages with cynical, morbid, or satirical comedy. The meme is designed for individuals who recognize the visual and textual references and can interpret the analogical mapping between the Joker character and the speaker's self-identification as 'twisted'."@en .
_:Nc59de838490c48e298bad3cbbcab5ee6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
_:Ncd25004049104f8abad7b05a58c6ca86 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01524#assertion_about_01524> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01524#mapping_001> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#prototypicalQuestions> "1. What written text appears in this meme? (Include all visible words, phrases, captions, and labels)\n\n2. How is the text positioned and formatted within the meme? (Consider placement, font size, color, style, and visual hierarchy)\n\n3. What is the tone and style of the written language used? (Formal/informal, serious/humorous, slang/standard, etc.)\n\n4. Are there any spelling, grammar, or punctuation choices that seem intentional? (Consider deliberate errors, unconventional capitalization, or punctuation for effect)\n\n5. What cultural references, phrases, or terminology are embedded in the text? (Identify idioms, pop culture references, internet slang, or specialized vocabulary)\n\n6. How does the text relate to or interact with the visual elements? (Does it complement, contradict, or add context to the images?)\n\n7. What assumptions does the text make about the audience's knowledge? (What background information or cultural literacy is assumed?)\n\n8. Are there multiple layers of meaning in the textual content? (Consider literal vs. implied meanings, wordplay, double entendres, or irony)\n\n9. What speech acts or communicative functions does the text perform? (Does it inform, persuade, mock, celebrate, complain, etc.?)\n\n10. How does the text contribute to the meme's overall message or humor? (What role does the written content play in achieving the meme's intended effect?)" .
_:Nbed8a9e4768a4ffeb4251289665b5937 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N1f376975abbf4626876eced508fa65c8 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01359#mapping_2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#AnalogicalMapping> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01436#school_shooter> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractedFrom> "01436.png" .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#superimposedOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:N1dd5592c799f4c279058a48cb17d1b2b .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01324#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: the_great_train_robbers; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image shows a group of seven older men standing outdoors, some holding books, with the text overlay 'the great train robbers'. The scene is presented in a black-and-white photograph, suggesting a historical or documentary tone. The men are dressed in casual to semi-formal attire, and their expressions range from neutral to smiling. The overall intent of the meme is to create a humorous or ironic juxtaposition by labeling a group of ordinary-looking men as 'the great train robbers', implying they are being portrayed as criminals or notorious figures. This is likely intended as a satirical or comedic meme, playing on the contrast between the mundane appearance of the men and the dramatic, criminal connotation of the text. There is no evidence of abusive, cyberbullying, harmful, hateful, misogynous, or propaganda content. The meme's intent is to provoke laughter or amusement through absurdity, not to target or harm any group. The visual elements (men holding books, casual clothing, urban setting) do not carry any toxic stereotypes or markers. The text overlay is the primary source of the meme's intent, mapping the image of ordinary men onto the label of infamous criminals, creating a humorous incongruity."@en .
