<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01452#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the visual material of the chimp, whose relaxed, slightly slumped posture with one arm draped over its body and the other resting on its knee is humorously equated to the feeling after a long day at work. This projection is implicit, as the meme uses the chimp as a universal symbol for human exhaustion, allowing the viewer to see themselves in the chimp's state. The text 'the feeling after a long day at work' explicitly anchors this projection by directly linking the chimp's visual state to a common human experience, making the user feel that the chimp embodies their own post-work fatigue. There are no explicit deictic pronouns like 'me' or 'you', but the analogical mapping creates an implicit 'you' projection, where the viewer identifies with the chimp's condition."@en .
