<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perimeterFraming> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:N097b411039db4878bfa5528f904b02d2 .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#pointingAdjacentTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:N26a6e4fa38834cb8bc6dafdf9e0c51ba .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#kermit_the_frog_character> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01456#Jewish_children_in_Holocaust> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The tragic fate of Jewish children during the Holocaust, who were often victims of genocide, forced labor, and medical experiments. The bottom image shows these children, emphasizing the innocence lost and the scale of the atrocity."@en .
_:N326b2b24d0ca492da42779bd2b930931 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01392#phone_call_scene> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "girl_talking_on_phone"@en .
_:N9d1e5616449a4dbb8233652d1dbd2e1f <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:N5b9c11df9d8a4737a0d4120e15a6765c <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N24a373b8080b44e1953828a4b78b79d3 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01469#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the overall violent metaphor scene, where the act of punching a Muslim child is equated with defusing a bomb. The user is implicitly positioned as the one performing the violent act ('you punch the muslim kid'), and the scene (the soldier, the text, the environment) serves as the context in which this act is framed as heroic or necessary. The user is not explicitly addressed with 'you' or 'me' in the text, but the first-person imperative and the violent, dehumanizing nature of the text create a direct projection of the user onto the aggressor role within the metaphor."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01524#dark_comedy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dark comedy"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01276#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the visual material of the smiling child, who serves as the focal point and symbolic representation of the idiom 'a smile is worth a thousand words'. The user identifies with the child's joyful expression as the universal, powerful, non-verbal communication that the text describes. The projection is implicit, not deictic, but the user is encouraged to see themselves in the child's smile as the idealized expression of the meme's core message."@en .
_:N6225284edd1c41e79a5c5c5571b06eca <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:Nd667ed39b43d4d5ca4c56681d59eeb7c <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:N0defcd4daf5e499eaed2b3c2612d6ad7 .
