<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01274#pados_wale_pandit_jee> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> .
_:N98991a210e61441b803920ab49ff0748 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Perspectival> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01476#mapping_id_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The textual phrase 'im confident in my own skin' is directly juxtaposed with the visual of a young woman wearing prosthetic arms, standing confidently. This creates an analogical mapping where the woman, as a visual entity, embodies the concept expressed in the text. Her confident pose, self-assured expression, and the prominent display of her prosthetic arms serve as a visual representation of the message of self-acceptance and empowerment. The mapping is explicit: the woman is the 'me' in the text, projecting her identity and confidence onto the viewer as an example of embracing one's unique physicality."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01245#audience_id_001> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme targets parents who have experienced or are aware of social or cultural tensions around children's participation in religious events like nativity plays, particularly when the child's attire or behavior is perceived as inappropriate or non-conforming. The text 'my son' and the context of exclusion from a nativity play directly address this demographic, who may relate to the humorous or ironic commentary on social norms surrounding religious inclusivity."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01247#mapping_1> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractionTimestamp> "2025-12-12T01:33:56.836112" .
