<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01276#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: a_smile_is_worth_a_thousand_words; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The scene depicts a young, smiling child in a blue shirt against a blurred green outdoor background. The text overlay states 'a smile is worth a thousand words,' conveying a positive, universal message about the power of non-verbal communication and emotional expression. There are no visual markers of toxicity, such as discriminatory physical appearance, harmful behavior, or contextual elements that suggest abuse, cyberbullying, or hate. The meme is intended to be uplifting and inspirational, using the universally positive image of a child's smile to analogically represent the idea that a simple expression can convey more than lengthy explanations. The intent is to promote positivity, empathy, and the value of emotional connection, with no harmful or propagandistic elements present."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#selectivelyMasking> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N8c2b6d7db4a14f3c91c533b092e1c228 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01469#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The target community is individuals who are exposed to or consume hate speech and propaganda that promotes violence against Muslims, particularly those who may be influenced by or sympathetic to extremist ideologies that equate Muslim identity with terrorism or inherent threat. The meme is designed to appeal to or normalize such views by using violent, racist, and dehumanizing language and imagery, targeting those who might be receptive to or reinforce harmful stereotypes about Muslims and Muslim children."@en .
