<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01492#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the visual material of the woman standing on the beach wearing a visor. The meme's humor and irony arise from the user identifying with or imagining themselves in the situation described by the text — 'i don't like the sun so i wear a visor' — and seeing the woman as a representation of that behavior. The projection is implicit, not explicit (no deictic pronouns like 'me' or 'you'), but the user is invited to mentally map themselves onto the woman's actions and appearance to understand the joke. The user is projected onto the 'woman' entity, which is the central visual material and the subject of the textual mapping."@en .
_:N9821265174cf47f7930f1ddd4d6a42ee <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:Na4b66a15260a4535bb17ede1243aa404 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Textual Material elements from the image. Textual Material includes: captions, text overlays, speech bubbles, written words, phrases, sentences, and any visible text. For each textual element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'main_caption', 'speech_bubble_text'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the main caption', 'the speech bubble text'), 3) A brief description of the text content and its context. Focus on all visible text elements that contribute to the meme's meaning. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"main_caption\", \"label\": \"the main caption\", \"description\": \"The text 'This is fine' written in bold letters at the bottom of the image\"}"@en .
