<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01472#mapping_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The text 'in my country this headdress is common to wear if you want to.' and 'here in other countries i am also free to wear it if i want to' implicitly maps the visual entity 'woman' to the speaker 'me'. The woman in the image is presented as the subject of the statement, representing the speaker's personal experience and perspective on wearing the hijab across different cultural contexts. This is an analogical mapping where the visual representation of the woman serves as the instantiation of the speaker 'me' in the text."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semiTransparentOverlaidOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N00924b37ef254e8aabe5491974472b1e .
_:N1a06a19ea64a4f3c801dfa6978b93559 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01469#stereotype_muslim_kids_as_vulnerable_targets> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme targets Muslim children specifically, implying they are 'bombs' that must be 'defused' by violent means. This is a grotesque and dehumanizing stereotype that reduces a child's humanity to a threat to be eliminated. It is rooted in the broader racist and Islamophobic trope that Muslim children are inherently dangerous or need to be controlled through violence."@en .
