<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#muslim_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme relies on the implicit association of 'definitely not a Muslim' with Kermit the Frog to mock or dismiss Muslims. This leverages a harmful stereotype that reduces a religious group to a caricature by juxtaposing them with a non-Muslim character. The stereotype is not visible in the image but is essential to understanding the meme's intent and toxicity."@en .
_:N65b5fa3896e245bea0205d6b646d2294 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
_:N45a84f80a4a5472e88755c25766800b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01327#Psychological_intensity> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relatedTo> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01327#joker_monologue> .
_:Nb37bce2c07dd4e31919911756361a03f <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01274#assertion_about_01274> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01274#pados_wale_pandit_jee> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#squirrel_behavior_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Squirrels are often associated with being 'nuts' in popular culture and idiomatic expressions, such as 'nuts' meaning crazy or eccentric. This association is not literal but is a cultural stereotype that makes the pun in the meme effective. The image of the squirrel is used to visually represent this stereotype."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01256#dark_humor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Humor derived from morbid, tragic, or taboo subjects. The overall intent of the meme is to create dark humor by juxtaposing a sad dog with a distressing scenario. This is a cultural phenomenon and social trend that helps explain the meme's intent and reception."@en .
