<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01524#violent_retaliation_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme relies on the stereotype that individuals, particularly men, may resort to extreme violence (e.g., pulling out a gun) as a response to perceived betrayal or infidelity. This reflects a harmful cultural trope that normalizes or trivializes the use of lethal force in personal relationships, which can be dangerous and is often associated with toxic masculinity."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01349#assertion_about_01349> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01349#Adolf_Hitler> .
_:N94fbe5a7177f4ee6bc5aa5f2dbbf3731 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N02440396a95f45909adeb2d14f01217a .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01284#stereotype_muslims_pigs> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This is a harmful and false stereotype that equates Muslims with pigs, often used to dehumanize and incite hatred against the Muslim community. The pig is a symbol of impurity in many Islamic traditions, and associating Muslims with pigs is a common trope in hate speech and Islamophobic rhetoric. This stereotype is not based on any factual or religious truth but is used to promote prejudice and discrimination."@en .
