<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01349#stereotype_historical_revisionism> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme implicitly references the concept of historical revisionism — the act of reinterpreting or rewriting history to fit a particular narrative, often to downplay or justify past atrocities. By pairing Hitler with a nurturing, childlike interaction, the meme attempts to rewrite his character, which is a dangerous and misleading form of revisionism."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01379#interaction_between_outsider_and_african_child> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A white American teenage girl is interacting with a young Black African child in a rural or impoverished setting. The girl is looking down at the child with a gentle expression, suggesting a caring or compassionate interaction. The child is looking up at the girl with a neutral or slightly curious expression. The scene is framed by text that labels the girl as 'American teenage girl' and the child as part of 'the children of Africa', which contextualizes the interaction as a 'visit' from an outsider. The visual contrast in skin tone and the juxtaposition of the two subjects activate stereotypical associations, such as the 'white savior' narrative, which implies a power imbalance and exoticizes the African child."@en .
