<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01235#habibi> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractedFrom> "01235.png" .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01395#mapping_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#AnalogicalMapping> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01524#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_file_name; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a person in casual attire (black hoodie and jeans) holding a handgun, with the text overlay 'when you catch your goat with another man.' The scene is framed to focus on the weapon and the act of concealment, suggesting a violent or retaliatory response. The overall intent is to convey a humorous or exaggerated threat in response to infidelity, using the visual of a gun to symbolize extreme, dangerous consequences. This meme relies on the juxtaposition of the mundane (a 'goat' as a metaphor for a partner) with the violent (a gun), creating a darkly comedic effect. While the meme is not explicitly abusive, hateful, or propagandistic toward specific groups, it contains harmful content by normalizing or trivializing the use of violence as a response to betrayal. The presence of the weapon, combined with the context of infidelity, can be seen as promoting a culture of aggression and potentially dangerous behavior, which may be harmful to individuals or communities that perceive such threats as realistic or justified. The meme does not target any specific protected characteristic (race, gender, etc.) but rather uses a universal metaphor for betrayal, making it less cyberbullying or misogynistic in nature, though its underlying message of violence is inherently harmful."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#opaqueOverlaidOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:Nc87dd22770bb44dea8898dafd98dd652 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01268#top_text> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01275#assertion_about_01275> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01275#audience_id> .
_:N1365224506e24e61a9f1adb51db9ca01 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01327#Cynicism> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A skeptical or pessimistic outlook, often expressed humorously. The meme's message reflects a cynical view of societal attempts to 'fix' or 'cure' people, suggesting that such attempts are futile or inappropriate for certain identities."@en .
_:Nc04877a54c9a40bea9b7b36e9a82884d <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01423#two_nude_individuals> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Two individuals are lying together on a bed, both nude. One person is positioned on their back with their head resting on a pillow, while the other is curled up on top of them, partially covering their face. Their bodies are in close physical contact, suggesting intimacy or rest. The scene is set in a private, restful environment with white linens."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#backgroundPreserving> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N65a78eb063674437b11c605f3369b2e6 .
_:N8f0421c0871043bba8a385c4ef2f47b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01243#dog_praise_culture> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme relies on the common cultural practice of pet owners praising their dogs with phrases like 'good girl' or 'good boy' as a form of positive reinforcement. This is a widely understood interaction in human-animal relationships, especially in Western cultures where dogs are often anthropomorphized and treated as family members. The humor comes from imagining the dog's self-aware, joyful response to this praise, which is a culturally constructed concept not directly visible in the image."@en .
_:Ndd1e552734e042e5a35fc83a4b04334e <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
