_:N27d0076dfd18452d8c93eb05a71d7d4b <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#kermit_not_muslim_mapping> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme uses the visual entity 'Kermit_the_Frog' as a stand-in for the textual concept 'definitely not a Muslim'. The text explicitly states that Kermit is 'definitely not a Muslim', creating an analogical mapping where the fictional character is projected onto the religious identity to mock or dismiss Muslims. This mapping is central to the meme's harmful intent, as it leverages Kermit's non-Muslim, fictional nature to stereotype and dehumanize Muslims."@en .
_:N9796694295ea495196290deb9d8e7a64 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01364#toxicity_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Harmful"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01247#dimension_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "the overallintent"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01498#Rhetorical_Question> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A question asked not to elicit an answer but to make a point or provoke thought. The bottom text uses a rhetorical question to challenge the perceived inconsistency in moral accountability, requiring understanding of rhetorical devices in political communication."@en .
_:N2c17277a0c1d4192845764d56ac7edcd <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N2d337dbd006f43fdbad2778b71e972b5 .
_:N4385d2e929b84b3d9bc1ec0b274cdeb6 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:Ncc73dee69b0e460588134b3eb241c59e .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01324#assertion_about_01324> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#hasToxicityType> "Harmful"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01245#meme_object_01245> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#hasAssertion> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01245#assertion_about_01245> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01459#top_text> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "text location"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01349#semiotic_projection_2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the scene depicting Adolf_Hitler in an affectionate interaction with the young girl. The text overlays ('girl you're so special', 'i'm gonna give you a gold star') create a narrative where the viewer is implicitly invited to participate in or witness this interaction. The user is projected as the recipient of the affectionate gesture, which is a dangerous projection that normalizes Hitler's monstrous legacy by framing him as a nurturing figure. This projection is reinforced by the intimate spatial arrangement and the direct address in the text."@en .
