_:N645e1516e08a40f5b0412a9205bd60ec <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01526#absurd_charge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The text 'that will be $0.15' for the act of 'putting the money in a bag' is a satirical exaggeration. This humor relies on the real-world concept of pricing and the expectation that such a trivial action should not incur a charge, making the joke only understandable to those familiar with retail pricing norms."@en .
_:N556fa7611cdf47dfb1ea41113c3493d2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N2e80acaf21df46d4a69d8f9ba378f231 .
_:N3d1515ee5e9a4acca2f76cc90c05f9f2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01275#palony_and_viannas> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The text references 'palony' and 'viannas', which are likely misspellings of names or entities (possibly 'Palo' and 'Vianna'). Without context, these are ambiguous, but in meme culture, they often refer to specific people or figures being dismissed or mocked. The background knowledge required is the recognition that these are likely names of individuals or entities that are being referenced in a satirical or confrontational manner."@en .
_:N78e3d909265a4ab0bba6db54b2cd9494 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:Nfd894883bf194dafb8278b828dd5debd <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:N651341b3e2e6404dbc80cd478bf90900 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01467#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "User projected onto the man as a 'honky'"@en .
_:Ne9757aa5f01e420d8fc12e2646edd6fb <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01236#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: isis_strip_club_meme; hasOverallIntent: harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme juxtaposes an image of sheep with their butts facing forward (a common internet meme) with an image of men in traditional Middle Eastern attire reacting with exaggerated shock and amusement, overlaid with the text 'meanwhile at the ISIS strip club'. The intent is to create a crude, mocking, and sexually suggestive joke by implying that ISIS members are watching a strip show, which is both offensive and harmful. It relies on harmful stereotypes by associating ISIS with sexualized, vulgar, and inappropriate behavior, which is not only inaccurate but also dehumanizing and potentially inciting hatred or violence against a specific group. The meme uses visual and textual elements to mock and degrade a group associated with terrorism, which can contribute to a hostile environment and normalize harmful attitudes. The combination of the visual elements (men in traditional attire, exaggerated expressions) and the text creates a toxic and harmful message that targets a specific community based on their association with a terrorist organization, making it a clear example of harmful content."@en .
_:Nb4bdc2f2b873420892bc41e55804da9b <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N7efafa0c673347848ff62a563e96ecf4 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01258#contempt> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The woman's facial expression, combined with the act of smoking and the defiant posture, conveys a sense of contempt or disdain, particularly towards the societal norms or expectations she is challenging. Her gaze and the smoky atmosphere reinforce this emotion."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01243#self_awareness_in_dogs> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractedFrom> "01243.png" .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#self_deprecating_joke> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme uses self-deprecating humor by implying that the viewer (or the squirrel) is 'a special kinda nuts,' which is a lighthearted, non-harmful way to acknowledge eccentricity or odd behavior. This type of humor relies on social understanding of humor and the acceptability of making light of one's own quirks."@en .
_:N8afdbcafee5b4139b00fcd15a9448745 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
_:N9d169fd2a2e647108ac69fac8c4a1b00 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#motionBlurIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N10e7acaf9f5c4abea48cbe0504536705 .
_:N9b1ee72237ce40b19c2f721d87bdd333 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:N824249ba655a4b858dfa92d98e81ac36 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01392#religious_invocation_concept> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The phrase 'hello jesus' is a modern, colloquial form of addressing Jesus Christ, often used in Christian contexts to express gratitude, seek divine intervention, or acknowledge a miracle. This concept is rooted in Christian theology and popular culture, where phrases like 'Jesus, thank you' or 'Jesus, make a way' are common in prayer or casual speech among believers. The phrase 'thanks for making a way!' further reinforces this by referencing the biblical concept of divine intervention or providence, often associated with overcoming obstacles or achieving something impossible."@en .
