<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01329#cowboys_ready_for_action> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Two older men in cowboy attire preparing for confrontation"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01379#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: american_teenage_girl_visiting_the_children_of_africa; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a white American teenage girl interacting with a young Black African child in a rural or impoverished setting. The text overlay frames the scene as a 'visit' to 'the children of Africa,' which is a common trope in Western media that often reduces African children to passive recipients of charity or pity, ignoring their agency and complex realities. This framing can be seen as patronizing and potentially harmful, as it may reinforce stereotypes of African children as helpless or in need of rescue by outsiders. The visual contrast in skin tone and the juxtaposition of the 'American teenage girl' with the 'children of Africa' can activate stereotypical associations, such as the 'white savior' narrative, which can be problematic as it may imply a power imbalance and exoticize the African child. The overall intent of the meme is likely to evoke an emotional response (e.g., pity, guilt, or inspiration) by highlighting a perceived disparity between the two subjects, potentially encouraging viewers to support charitable causes or feel morally obligated to help. While not overtly abusive or hateful, the meme's intent can be considered harmful due to its potential to perpetuate simplistic, stereotypical narratives about Africa and its people, which can contribute to a distorted understanding of global inequality and cultural contexts. The meme may also be propagandistic in nature, subtly encouraging viewers to support a particular narrative or cause without critically examining the underlying assumptions or power dynamics involved."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#colorMatchedIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
