_:Ndb4d284d6c6c433ba026b2af9fb24740 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01497#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is not explicitly projected onto any deictic pronoun like 'me' or 'you'. However, the meme's structure and intent create an implicit projection where the viewer (user) is positioned as someone who is either endorsing, mocking, or identifying with the stereotype being presented. The user is projected onto the visual material of the man holding the child, which is then semiotically mapped to the harmful stereotype 'married to children'. This projection is not direct (no 'you' or 'me'), but it is inferable from the meme's function: the user is invited to consume and interpret the image within the context of the stereotype, thereby internalizing or aligning with the meme's framing. The user is projected onto the 'man' visual material, which is the central subject of the stereotype."@en .
_:N1a7558a1192440ceb8cb885cd7196bde <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:Nf9b3f8f5e7cb4582b8174825b1531109 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#metaphoricallyMapsTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates metaphorical or analogical mapping between dimension instances"@en .
_:Nc4b799953c5c4b3ba0cacb803287e83e <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:N03ca2fceea9b4173892014d4ec310674 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01245#meme_juxtaposition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A comedic or ironic technique in memes that involves placing two contrasting or incongruous elements together to create humor or commentary. In this case, the juxtaposition of a child in military attire with the context of a nativity play is used to create a humorous commentary on social norms or perceived inappropriateness."@en .
_:Nb1e58c898473457085701c7de963c4a6 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:N4f6b930d086c4ad1ab197d0350b541f1 .
_:N8b2756500d8c4c58bdc0e3071cde8972 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#is_holding> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01295#false_equivalence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme creates a false equivalence by equating the term 'transgender' with 'clergy' in the context of men wearing dresses and molesting children. This is a misleading and harmful comparison that is used to incite fear and hatred against both religious figures and transgender people. This is a form of hate speech and cyberbullying."@en .
