_:N38208c8fb39c441f87967e46ad7f0dea <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N8053178bb6874f30af24952029c7019f .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01476#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme is designed for individuals engaged in or interested in the Body Positivity Movement, which promotes self-acceptance and confidence regardless of physical appearance or disability. The visual of the woman with prosthetic arms confidently embracing her unique physicality directly aligns with this community's values and messaging."@en .
_:N1f2973856f5c46698024586c89225da8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:N2888f0766f344630aaa9ce3e388d40ed <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Perspective-aligned> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text following the perspective, depth, and vanishing points of memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#ContextualReferentAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#SemanticAdjacency> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01348#sheep> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#are_observed_by> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01348#children> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01364#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts children dressed in white protective suits and head coverings, with one child wearing a vest resembling a bomb with red and black components. The text overlay reads, 'and that was the last nativity play my son was invited to take part in.' This juxtaposition creates a darkly ironic and potentially harmful message, implying that the nativity play was a 'bomb' or dangerous event, which is a violent and inappropriate metaphor for a children's performance. The visual elements, including the bomb-like vest and the children's attire, combined with the text, may be interpreted as promoting or normalizing violence, especially when associated with children. The meme uses the visual of children in a context that mimics terrorism or violence to make a satirical or critical point, but this can be perceived as harmful due to its potential to normalize or trivialize violence, particularly when it involves children. The image may also be seen as a form of propaganda or satire that could be offensive or triggering to certain audiences, especially those who associate such imagery with real-world violence or terrorism. The meme's intent is to shock or provoke a reaction by juxtaposing the innocence of a nativity play with the imagery of violence, which can be interpreted as harmful due to its potential to cause distress or promote negative associations."@en .
_:Ne8eab63ede6b42b9883e518423e51cd5 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:N18a57015e5114e51b1c008f910c1ca6b .
_:Nfd89fbaae908468282a687aa06e3cf9c <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:N2e5fd498dfa143d986b8521fac5be7c7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:Neb9b22160fa74dfba1427b4e81577040 .
_:Nacb1d38419e348bb8528243756e5f1e8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
_:N060999a1d6894adcb1993fb1e5c2f294 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01526#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_file_name; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a smiling cashier in a retail setting, holding a payment terminal, with overlaid text that reads 'put the money in a bag. now! that will be $0.15'. The scene is a common retail checkout scenario. The text is absurd and nonsensical, suggesting a joke or satire about the triviality of a transaction or the absurdity of being charged for something like 'putting money in a bag'. There is no indication of toxicity, harmfulness, abuse, cyberbullying, misogyny, or propaganda. The intent is purely humorous and satirical, using the juxtaposition of a mundane retail interaction with an illogical, exaggerated instruction to create comedic effect. The visual elements (cashier, store, payment terminal) are neutral and do not carry any stereotypical or harmful markers. The meme relies on the incongruity between the visual context and the text to generate humor, not on targeting or degrading any group or individual."@en .
_:Nd40618df57d642c9a2dfa2e8d92c69e6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N9063f3e604c543d799df78202b6ca01b .
