<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01364#political_propaganda> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The dissemination of information — often biased or misleading — to promote a particular political cause or point of view. The meme's visual and textual elements could be interpreted as a form of political propaganda, especially if used to critique or mock a specific group or ideology. Understanding this concept is necessary to interpret the potential intent behind the image."@en .
_:N7844fb9c96354236854d2fd2f1e80d83 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N221bea1160824860a5fef1992e73455e .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01483#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "User projected onto the central man as a representation of boredom"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01325#intense_screaming_reaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A man with a purple and white headscarf is captured mid-scream, mouth wide open showing teeth, eyes narrowed, conveying extreme emotional intensity. He is framed from the chest up against a bright blue sky, making his facial expression the central focus. The text overlay 'when your world cup team scores a goal' contextualizes the scene as a humorous representation of fan ecstasy."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01293#hygiene_consequences> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme humorously implies that forgetting to wash hands leads to a gross, unpleasant state (represented by the oily, dirty hand). This is based on real-world knowledge that unclean hands can harbor bacteria and germs, leading to illness or social discomfort. The visual of the dripping, greasy hand is a metaphor for the negative outcomes of poor hygiene."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#AnalogicalMapping> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Dimension> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01392#christian_cultural_reference> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The phrase 'hello jesus' and 'thanks for making a way!' are culturally specific to Christian communities, particularly in contexts where faith is used to explain or celebrate life events. This reference is not visible in the image but is essential to understanding the meme's humor and intent. It relies on the viewer's familiarity with Christian expressions of gratitude and faith, which are common in many parts of the world, especially in the United States and other Western countries."@en .
