<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01452#assertion_about_01452> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01452#text_chunk_1> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01465#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts individuals dressed in white robes and niqabs, with toy-like bomb props strapped to their waists, and text overlaid that reads 'there is difference between violating muslims and other muslims' and 'dont get confused'. The visual elements, including the niqab (a religious/cultural marker), the bomb props (symbolizing violence), and the text, are juxtaposed to create a misleading and potentially inflammatory message. The intent appears to be to provoke confusion or division by suggesting a distinction between 'violating' and 'other' Muslims, which could be interpreted as endorsing or normalizing violence against Muslims. This is harmful as it may incite prejudice, promote division within the Muslim community, and potentially encourage violence or hatred against Muslims. The use of the niqab as a qua-entity to represent 'Muslims' in a stereotypical and potentially dehumanizing way, combined with the violent imagery and divisive text, creates a toxic and harmful message. The meme is not abusive or cyberbullying in the direct sense, but it is harmful because it promotes a dangerous narrative that could lead to real-world harm, including violence or discrimination against Muslims. The overall intent is to manipulate perception and sow discord by exploiting religious and cultural markers in a misleading and potentially dangerous way."@en .
_:N11f1b16fe61b4ece82453548de603b84 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:N3bdeb00a83b54acb82598e37487f89c1 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01258#derogatory_term_faggot> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Derogatory term: "@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perimeterFraming> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "27"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01456#nazi_leaders_parade> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Scene> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01452#post_work_relaxation_culture> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme taps into the cultural norm of taking time to relax after work, often referred to as 'unwinding' or 'decompressing'. This concept is rooted in modern work-life balance discussions and is a widely recognized social behavior, especially in office and corporate environments."@en .
