<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#faceMasking> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:N1943ba0519224b2db7bdd7d1df8a75d8 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01236#harmful_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A widely held but inaccurate belief or generalization about a group of people that is often used to justify discrimination or prejudice. The meme relies on harmful stereotypes by associating ISIS with sexualized, vulgar, and inappropriate behavior, which is not only inaccurate but also dehumanizing and potentially inciting hatred or violence against a specific group. This stereotype is toxic and contributes to a hostile environment by normalizing harmful attitudes."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01483#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is implicitly projected onto the central man in the image, who embodies the feeling of boredom described in the text. The meme invites the viewer to identify with the central figure's disengaged state, making the text 'feeling bored, let's go to a mosque' a direct address to the user's own potential boredom. The visual of the bored man serves as a mirror for the user's internal state, creating a personal identification with the subject of the meme."@en .
